# What must never reach the host.
#
# The vercel cli uploads the working directory, and it does NOT reliably fall
# back to .gitignore, so an ignored file is not automatically an unshipped one.
# The first deploy proved that the hard way: .env went up, MAPVIS_SOLO came
# with it, and /api/me answered as the club account to a request carrying no
# cookie at all. Every anonymous visitor would have been signed in as ATC.
#
# Real configuration lives in vercel's own environment variables. This file
# exists so a local convenience can never become a hosted one.
.env
.env.*
!.env.example

# scratch. On a host this is /tmp and is rebuilt per request; shipping a
# laptop's copy would be megabytes of pngs that get ignored anyway.
work/
public/work/

# the previous generation's dead weight
archive/
.vite-cache/
.tmp-refute/
dist-ssr/

# the imaging toolkit: standalone, nothing in the request path imports it, and
# it is a megabyte the host would upload on every deploy for nothing
server/imaging/

# history and tooling the runtime never reads
.git/
.github/
docs/
reference/
*.blend
*.blend1
__pycache__/
